Beni Capital lets users invest in creative projects (theatre / film / TV / music). The Easy API integration exists to enable real-money investment payments — it is taking the role Stripe usually plays.

**Why:** The user is building out a payment flow and chose Easy API over Stripe; payment endpoints are the primary driver of this integration, not generic CRUD.

**How to apply:**
- When the user shares a new Easy API endpoint, treat it as part of the invest → pay → confirm → record flow first; only fall back to generic CRUD framing if it clearly isn't payment-related.
- A new endpoint typically lands changes across multiple layers: `lib/easy-api/*` (domain method), `types/project.d.ts` or `types/transaction.ts` (new fields), `firestore.rules` + `lib/firebase/*-actions.ts` (data shape + rules), and the dashboard UI (`app/(dashboard)/`, `modules/dashboard/`). Walk this list and ask which layers move — don't just wire the HTTP call.
- Full integration brief lives in [EASY_API_INTEGRATION.md](EASY_API_INTEGRATION.md) at repo root — read it before starting endpoint work. The Easy API service itself is at `lib/easy-api/server.ts`.
- Firebase Auth still owns user identity; the Easy API server key is a backend secret. Client never calls Easy API directly — always through `app/api/*` route handlers that verify the Firebase ID token first.

**Investment tiers (product decision, 2026-05-01):** Every project accepts the same 6 fixed tiers — $5k, $10k, $25k, $50k, $100k, $250k. Implemented as **global** Easy API entities: 1 Product + 6 Prices, created once per environment via a bootstrap script, IDs stored in env vars (`EASY_API_PRICE_ID_5K` … `_250K`). No per-project Easy API entities — the tier is shared across all projects. Payment-link is always `amount_type: 'FIXED'` with `adjustable: false`, and `tags.projectId` binds the payment to the specific project.
